(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-12-12"] = {"5.1":{"s":"The definite integral of a continuous, non-negative function f over [a, b] is the area under the curve y = f(x) above the x-axis, between the lines x = a and x = b. Drawing a rough sketch of the curves first shows which region is meant and which limits to use. Areas are always positive, and are given in square units.","f":[{"x":"A = ∫_a^b f(x) dx","n":"Area bounded by y = f(x) >= 0, the X axis and the lines x = a, x = b.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.178"}],"w":["Sketch the region first: if the curve dips below the axis, split the integral there."],"x":["The definite integral gives area. If f(x) is continuous and not negative on [a, b], the area bounded by the curve y = f(x), the x-axis and the lines x = a and x = b is the integral of f(x) dx from a to b. Think of thin vertical strips of height f(x) and width dx. Adding up all the strips gives the integral.","Steps for any area problem: (1) draw a rough sketch of the curve and the region; (2) decide which strips to use, vertical or horizontal; (3) find the limits, using the given lines or the points where curves meet; (4) integrate and put in the limits; (5) write the answer in square units.","Areas are always positive. If part of the curve is below the x-axis, the integral for that part is negative. Find the area of each part separately, split at the points where the curve crosses the axis, and add their absolute values. The integral over the whole range would partly cancel, and would not be the area.","Useful areas: the circle x² + y² = a² has area πa². The ellipse x²/a² + y²/b² = 1 has area πab. By symmetry you can find the area of one quarter and multiply by 4."],"e":"The area under y = x² from x = 0 to x = 3 is the integral of x² dx = [x³/3] from 0 to 3 = 9 square units. The area under one arch of y = sin x, from 0 to π, is [−cos x] = 2 square units."},"5.2":{"s":"The area bounded by a curve, an axis and given lines is found by integrating along the other axis when the curve is given as x in terms of y: integrate x dy between y = c and y = d, for the region next to the Y axis. For a curve below the axis the integral is negative, so take its absolute value. A region partly above and partly below the axis is split into parts.","f":[{"x":"A = ∫_c^d x dy","n":"Area between the curve x = g(y) and the Y axis, for heights y from c up to d.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.179"}],"w":["Area is never negative: below the axis take the numerical value of that part."],"x":["Sometimes it is easier to use horizontal strips. If the curve is given as x = g(y), and the region touches the y-axis and lies between the heights c and d, add up horizontal strips: the area is the integral of x dy, with y going from c to d. The limits are values of y, and each strip has length x and thickness dy.","Choosing the strips: use vertical strips (integrate dx) when the curve is easily written as y in terms of x. Use horizontal strips (integrate dy) when x in terms of y is simpler, or when the region is bounded by the y-axis. A parabola such as y² = 4x opens to the side, and is easiest with horizontal strips, or with symmetry about the x-axis.","For the area between the curve y = x² and the y-axis, up to the line y = 4, the horizontal strips have length x = √y. The area is the integral of √y dy from 0 to 4, which is 16/3. Check with vertical strips: the area of the rectangle 4 × 2 = 8 minus the area under the curve (8/3), which is also 16/3.","If the curve is on the left of the y-axis, the integral of x dy is negative, so take the numerical value. If the region crosses the x-axis, split it into parts and add the areas. Common mistake: using x-limits with a dy integral."],"e":"Area between the parabola x = y² and the line x = 4. They meet at y = ±2. Using horizontal strips, A = the integral of (4 − y²) dy from −2 to 2 = 2 × (8 − 8/3) = 32/3 square units."},"5.3":{"s":"The area between two curves y = f(x) and y = g(x) is the difference of the areas under them. First find where they meet by solving the two equations together; these points give the limits a and b. Then subtract the area under the lower curve from the area under the upper one, and take the absolute value.","f":[{"x":"A = |∫_a^b f(x) dx - ∫_a^b g(x) dx|","n":"Area between the curves y = f(x) and y = g(x), from their points of intersection x = a to x = b.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.180"}],"w":["Find the points of intersection before integrating; they are the limits."],"x":["To find the area between two curves y = f(x) and y = g(x), find the area under each and subtract. The strip at x has height f(x) − g(x), the upper curve minus the lower curve. The area is the absolute value of the integral of [f(x) − g(x)] dx from a to b.","Steps: (1) sketch both curves; (2) solve f(x) = g(x) to find the points where they meet, which give the limits a and b; (3) decide which curve is above, by testing a value of x between a and b; (4) integrate the upper minus the lower; (5) write the answer in square units.","If the curves cross inside the region, the upper and lower curves change places. Split the integral at the crossing point, and add the absolute values of the parts. If a region has a line of symmetry, find half of it and double the result.","Common mistakes: taking the limits from the given lines when the region is closed by the curves themselves, subtracting in the wrong order and getting a negative answer, and forgetting the intersection points."],"e":"Area between y = x and y = x². They meet where x = x², at x = 0 and x = 1. In between, the line is above the parabola. Area = the integral of (x − x²) dx from 0 to 1 = 1/2 − 1/3 = 1/6 square unit."}};
